00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.040
Covid-19 is a devious disease.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:18.410
It’s not deadly enough to kill a large proportion
of its hosts, so they go on to spread it to

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:23.460
an incredible number of individuals, meaning
its fairly low mortality rate has already

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.990
killed hundreds of thousands.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:31.580
It disguises itself, presenting like a common
cold, yet can escalate to something far more

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:32.580
worrisome.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.940
It also hides, with the possibility of being
spread by someone who does not exhibit any

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.890
symptoms whatsoever.

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:44.970
All together, this leads to one simple truth:
until there is an effective treatment, a vaccine,

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:49.670
or herd immunity, perhaps the most important
thing a healthcare system itself can do to

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:53.710
minimize the number of cases and deaths is
test.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:58.899
Worldwide, diagnostic testing for Covid-19
has ramped up incredibly fast.

00:00:58.899 --> 00:01:03.399
The first genetic sequence of the virus was
published on January 5th, and within days,

00:01:03.399 --> 00:01:06.490
the first testing protocols were published
by researchers.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:10.709
In the second half of January, the world was
testing hundreds of individuals per day.

00:01:10.709 --> 00:01:15.649
By February, they were testing thousands per
day, then in March, hundreds of thousands,

00:01:15.649 --> 00:01:21.939
and now, in July, 2020, millions of Coronavirus
tests are performed and processed each and

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:23.520
every day.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.030
Believe it or not, though, this is not enough.

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:31.639
The process has been mired in issues and,
to get to the point where testing can actually

00:01:31.639 --> 00:01:37.599
minimize the damage, rather than just quantify
it, we need to rethink the system itself.

00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:43.819
The core of any Covid test is a laboratory
method known as a polymerase chain reaction.

00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:48.999
This is a commonly-used procedure that copies
DNA or, through reverse transcription, RNA,

00:01:48.999 --> 00:01:50.579
enough so that it can be studied.

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:55.700
The various steps in the process—denaturation,
annealing, and elongation—require various

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:00.479
specific temperatures, and so performing this
process requires a machine called a thermal

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:01.479
cycler.

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:06.059
These thermal cyclers can reach precise temperatures
incredibly quickly and accurately, but it

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.780
comes at a cost.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.090
The cheapest thermal cyclers are priced in
the thousands of dollars, but the high-throughput

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:17.579
ones used to process COVID tests often cost
in the many tens of thousands of dollars.

00:02:17.579 --> 00:02:22.730
Therefore, at this time, it is not practical
for every doctor’s office to have one, especially

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.870
as many less-automated variants require trained
technicians to operate.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:30.909
There are some faster point-of-care tests
hitting the market, but they tend to be less

00:02:30.909 --> 00:02:35.010
accurate, more expensive, and can process
fewer tests at a time.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:40.110
Therefore, while samples can be collected
almost anywhere, processing them is a greater

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.340
challenge.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.900
One of the largest testing providers in the
US is a company called Quest Diagnostics.

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:52.090
At the time of writing, they have processed
about 17% of all tests performed in the US,

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.790
and have the capacity for 130,000 each day.

00:02:55.790 --> 00:03:00.010
They carry out this work in one of their twenty-eight
labs spread out across the United States.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:05.129
Of course, it’s easy to see that there are
wide swaths of the country without a lab nearby,

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:09.569
so, to get samples from where they’re collected
to where they’re processed, they rely on

00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:12.420
their own small logistics network.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:17.450
This system involves plenty of ground transportation,
but also, in order to quickly transport samples

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:21.340
from far-away areas to the lab, they use airplanes.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.900
Each night, after the day’s samples have
been collected around the country, Quest Diagnostic’s

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.730
fleet of airplanes get to sky starting at
around 7 pm.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:34.040
These twenty or so aircraft are primarily
based in Reading, Pennsylvania and Lawrenceville,

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.040
Georgia.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:42.209
As an example, on July 16th, 2020, one aircraft,
a Pilatus PC-12 registered as N338QD, took

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:48.260
off at 8:45 pm from Reading, Pennsylvania
and flew to Elmira, Syracuse, Rochester, Niagara

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.409
Falls, Allegheny County, Manassas, and Baltimore
where samples were trucked to the lab a few

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:54.389
miles from BWI airport.

00:03:54.389 --> 00:03:58.450
The aircraft then returned to Reading, Pennsylvania
by 3:30 am.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:02.529
On that night, the majority of their aircraft
focused their flights on the American south,

00:04:02.529 --> 00:04:03.529
though.

00:04:03.529 --> 00:04:08.560
This is a region without many Quest Diagnostics
labs and a surge of cases, at that time.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.360
Another PC-12, for example, flew from Lawrenceville,
Georgia to Jacksonville, Gainesville, Tampa,

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.630
Orlando, Valdosta, Lawrenceville, Roanoke,
Manassas, and Greensboro, before returning

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.780
to Lawrenceville, all in one night.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:23.970
Filling in the rest of the routes, you can
see just how much ground this small airline

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:28.639
covered in one night—stopping at a large
number of cities and towns, going to just

00:04:28.639 --> 00:04:30.110
a few labs.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:35.821
Now, in theory, a system like this, bringing
a whole countries worth of samples to a few

00:04:35.821 --> 00:04:41.599
central sites, should provide economies and
efficiencies of scale, and, to an extent,

00:04:41.599 --> 00:04:47.470
it does, however, other economic concepts
have simultaneously decreased its effectiveness.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:53.330
To start with, there’s a heavy supply and
demand imbalance for crucial equipment.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:58.070
To perform a test, about twenty different
consumable supplies are needed including nasal

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:02.050
swabs, transport tubes, reagents, and other
equipment.

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:06.540
Without any of these twenty items, a test
cannot be performed, so a bottleneck in any

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.620
one of those twenty supply chains stops testing.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:14.729
In addition, economic forces are actually
decreasing overall efficiency.

00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:18.889
Particularly in a country like the US, where
testing is overwhelmingly conducted by private

00:05:18.889 --> 00:05:23.300
organizations rather than the government,
thousands of providers are each bidding against

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:25.120
each other for equipment.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.830
In conventional economics, one typically wants
to avoid monopolies and oligopolies because

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.080
a few number of players have too much power.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.979
This is why, in countries like the UK, for
example, where 75% of the grocery market is

00:05:37.979 --> 00:05:43.780
cornered by four firms, products like milk
are sold for just about what it costs to produce—farmers

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.379
barely turn a profit.

00:05:45.379 --> 00:05:49.820
That’s because, if a farmer can’t sell
their milk to Tesco, Sainsbury’s, Asda,

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:54.220
or Morrisons, they don’t really have another
option, so they’ll take any price they can

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:59.220
get and meanwhile, there are plenty of farmers,
so the grocery stores have plenty of options

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.669
if one says no to their low price.

00:06:01.669 --> 00:06:07.210
Meanwhile, in the Covid test supply market,
the producers have thousands upon thousands

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:12.759
of potential customers, the testing providers,
so if one customer won’t accept a high price,

00:06:12.759 --> 00:06:16.990
there are plenty of others and one that most
desperately needs the supplies inevitably

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:17.990
will.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:22.190
If, for example, the federal government was
the only customer in the US, or just the largest

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:26.580
one by far, the supply producers would just
have to accept what the government paid because

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.190
there would be no alternatives.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:33.879
Now, resources might not be allocated as efficiently
using a centrally-planned system, but it would

00:06:33.879 --> 00:06:36.150
at least come at a lower cost.

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:41.080
In the real world, the larger providers like
LabCorp and Quest Diagnostics do have higher

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.301
buying power, processing more tests per day
than most countries, but they still have to

00:06:45.301 --> 00:06:50.629
compete against the rest of the world in procuring
supplies, which increases cost.

00:06:50.629 --> 00:06:54.870
In recent weeks, Quest Diagnostics, and other
major testing providers, have been able to

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:59.500
collect far more samples than they have been
able to process, and tests now take up to

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:02.240
two weeks, or sometimes more, to come back.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:08.030
Tests that take so long to return are of little
use since many asymptomatic or mildly symptomatic

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:12.199
patients might not quarantine for that full
period while waiting for results.

00:07:12.199 --> 00:07:17.960
Therefore, major focus has been placed on
how to increase the efficiency of testing.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:23.120
For this, Quest Diagnostics received the first
emergency use authorization in the US from

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.990
the FDA to implement a technique known as,
“pooled testing.”

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:31.770
With this, up to four samples will be combined
and tested together.

00:07:31.770 --> 00:07:36.979
If they generate a negative result, then all
four tests are considered negative.

00:07:36.979 --> 00:07:40.800
If the group tests positive, then they are
separated and tested individually.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.960
Now, if 100 people are tested traditionally,
100 tests will have to be processed.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:49.500
But let’s say that 100 people are tested
using pooled testing.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:55.100
In the US, on average, about 8% of tests currently
come back positive, so with 100 tests, grouped

00:07:55.100 --> 00:08:00.689
into 25 pools of four, you would expect about
32% of those to come back positive.

00:08:00.689 --> 00:08:05.939
For those positive pools, you’d then process
each test individually, so you will have processed

00:08:05.939 --> 00:08:08.389
five times for four results.

00:08:08.389 --> 00:08:13.930
For the 17 pools that came back negative,
you only processed once, meaning that altogether,

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:19.789
this pooled testing technique used 57 processing
slots to generate 100 results—effectively

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:21.900
almost doubling capacity.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:26.230
This pooled testing technique, though, only
works if testing is widespread enough that

00:08:26.230 --> 00:08:29.849
only a small portion of results come back
positive.

00:08:29.849 --> 00:08:35.050
If an average of 20% of results came back
positive, as was the case in the US in early-April,

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:37.230
the efficiency would diminish quickly.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:42.420
In that case, you’d expect to have to re-test
20 of the 25 pools meaning that, overall,

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:47.830
you’d use 105 processing slots to generate
100 results—more than if you just tested

00:08:47.830 --> 00:08:49.280
them all individually.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:54.110
Therefore, this technique does not work as
well for testing in hotspots, where positivity

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:55.930
rates are far higher.

00:08:55.930 --> 00:09:01.140
Conversely, though, if positivity rates are
quite low, the efficiency of pooled testing

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:07.350
can increase as one could efficiently group
8 or 12 or 16 or more tests together.

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:11.480
Lower positivity rates can be achieved through
collecting more samples, so pooled sampling

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.040
can increase testing capacity, therefore increasing
its own efficiency.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:22.000
But no matter how fast PCR testing itself
is, it is always a lagging indicator.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:26.440
That is because it takes a number of days
for people to develop symptoms after exposure,

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:31.310
then more days for them to decide to get tested,
then more days for the test to come back.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:35.860
That means that, realistically, it will take
weeks for significant upticks in transmission

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:37.770
to show in the data.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:43.370
Therefore, some focus has been placed on developing
a more real-time indicator for COVID transmission.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:46.360
The solution for that is sewage.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.400
Sewage is naturally aggregated into a few
processing plants, so by testing it for the

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:54.810
presence of COVID’s genetic signature, one
can gain a more accurate picture of how a

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:59.510
whole area is doing, without the potential
sampling biases of clinical testing.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:04.420
This technique can show if an area has coronavirus
transmission, but it can also give a big-picture

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:06.370
view of how much.

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:11.540
Not only that, but in research tests, sewage
testing indicates spikes about a week before

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:15.560
clinical testing, meaning it can help tell
governments if they should increase restrictions

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:18.830
before it’s potentially too late to make
an impact.

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:21.580
But let’s go back to the pooled testing.

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:26.420
Using sewage testing, you can know the relative
chance of a single person from a given area

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:31.410
of having Coronavirus, so imagine if you could
combine the sewage testing and clinical pooled

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.980
testing into one cohesive system.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:37.420
This technique was proposed by a group of
researchers from Berkley.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:41.950
If you know that, say, there was a 25% chance
of a test from Florida coming back positive,

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:45.990
you would know that it would be more efficient
to test that on its own, however, if you knew

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:49.800
that there was a 1% chance that a test from
North Dakota came back positive, you could

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:55.440
pool that test that with a large group of
other low-risk tests and save processing slots.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:11:00.190
You could also, for example, know that a sample
from a healthcare or retail worker would have

00:11:00.190 --> 00:11:04.480
a much higher chance of coming back positive
than a test of an businessperson working from

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:05.480
home.

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:10.240
The Berkley research proposes a system of
machine learning to scale pooled testing sizes

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.720
based on risk factors.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:16.930
Low-risk samples would be grouped into large
pools, and high-risk samples would be grouped

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:21.810
into smaller pools or tested alone so that
pooled testing never leads to an efficiency

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:24.730
disadvantage, no longer the positivity rate.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:30.210
The true genius of pooled testing is that
efficiency increases as test numbers increase,

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:35.230
so it only takes twice the resources to test
a population once daily as it does to test

00:11:35.230 --> 00:11:36.660
once monthly.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.130
Hypothetically, a pooled testing system designed
by machine learning could lower the cost of

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:46.780
a test to just $3 to $5, meaning the frequency
of testing could increase dramatically.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:53.080
Now, we already know when the crunch time
will be for testing—Fall, 2020.

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:57.800
Sometime between October and December, each
year, there begins a massive increase in the

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:01.400
number of people catching the flu, which is
highly seasonal.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:05.920
Covid-19’s symptoms closely mirror those
of the flu, therefore there will be a massive

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:09.080
increase in people exhibiting Covid symptoms.

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:13.230
Those people will want to get tested, to rule
out the possibility of Coronavirus, which

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:18.300
means demand will be higher than ever for
testing, even if real infection numbers are

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:19.300
lower.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:23.330
All the supplies for testing produced now
are being used now, there is no stash being

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:27.660
built up, which means testing providers and
healthcare systems worldwide have a hefty

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:34.220
task at hand if there is to be any hope of
testing capacity matching demand in the coming

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:35.220
winter.

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.990
Like so many things going on right now, Covid
testing logistics and its issues are strongly

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:44.180
rooted in economic theory, and there’s plenty
more I could talk about, but this video was

